    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to clean up my quickbooks for the year. i bought a really expensive ergonomic chair for my home studio like two months ago. should i be categorizing that as a business expense or is that considered a personal draw since it's in my house?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is a very common dilemma for home-based entrepreneurs, but the answer is actually quite straightforward for a quickbooks cleanup. Since you bought the chair specifically for your home studio, it is definitely a **business expense** (specifically, a capital asset or furniture), not a personal draw. The fact that it's in your house doesn't make it personal; it just means you need to track the location carefully.\n\nIn QuickBooks, you should categorize this under **Furniture & Fixtures** or a custom **Office Equipment** category, and then run your \"Cost of Goods Sold\" or \"Depreciation\" reports to see how it affects your tax write-offs. If you accidentally coded it as a personal draw, you'll just have to reverse that transaction and re-enter it as a business purchase, which is a hassle but not a disaster.\n\nJust remember, if you have a separate room or a dedicated desk area, you can often deduct 100% of the cost. If you're working from the same room as your bedroom, you might need to prorate the expense based on the square footage, which is a bit more tedious but still worth it.\n\nIt's a good move to get this sorted out",
      "source": "target",
